// FRONTEND (runs in the browser, fully visible/modifiable by the user)
async function placeOrder(cartItems) {
  const response = await fetch('/api/orders', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ items: cartItems })
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Order failed with status ${response.status}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
// The frontend's job here: collect the cart data, send the request,
// handle the response or error for display. It does NOT calculate
// the final price, check stock, or verify payment - those happen
// only on the backend, where they cannot be tampered with.

// BACKEND (runs on the server, hidden from and not modifiable by the user)
// (conceptual pseudocode, before Express syntax is introduced next lesson)
//
// when a POST /api/orders request arrives:
//   1. verify the user is actually logged in (authentication)
//   2. look up the REAL, current price of each item from the database
//      (never trust a price sent from the frontend)
//   3. check actual current stock levels
//   4. calculate the real total server-side
//   5. verify payment
//   6. save the order to the database
//   7. send back a response confirming success or describing what failed

sections.content:
Running a JavaScript file with Node.js from the terminal:
node app.js

Node.js uses a module system for splitting code across multiple files, similar in spirit to the import/export syntax from Module 6, with two coexisting styles in modern Node.js:

CommonJS (the original, still extremely common in existing Node.js code):
// math.js
function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
module.exports = { add };

// app.js
const { add } = require('./math');
console.log(add(2, 3)); // 5

ES Modules (the same import/export syntax from Module 6, also supported in modern Node.js with the right configuration):
// math.js
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }

// app.js
import { add } from './math.js';

Node.js also includes npm (Node Package Manager), the tool used to install third-party packages — npm install express, which you'll use in the very next lesson, downloads and makes available the Express framework, along with a package.json file that records exactly which packages a given project depends on.

