Basic manual validation inside a route handler, before lesson 8 introduces middleware as a cleaner way to organize this same logic:

app.post('/tasks', (req, res) => {
  const { title } = req.body;

  if (!title || typeof title !== 'string' || title.trim() === '') {
    return res.status(400).json({
      success: false,
      error: 'A non-empty title is required'
    });
  }

  const newTask = { id: nextId++, title: title.trim(), completed: false };
  tasks.push(newTask);
  res.status(201).json({ success: true, data: newTask });
});

This checks three things: that title was actually provided at all (truthy check), that it's genuinely a string (type check — recall Module 4's discussion of why typeof matters), and that it isn't just empty or whitespace once trimmed. Status 400 ('Bad Request', from Module 1's HTTP lesson) is the conventional status code for exactly this situation: the request reached the server fine, but the data it contained was invalid in some way that's the client's responsibility to fix.

