sections.content:
Express recognizes a special kind of middleware specifically for handling errors, distinguished by having FOUR parameters instead of three (err, req, res, next), and registered AFTER all your normal routes and middleware:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error('Error caught:', err.message);
  res.status(err.status || 500).json({
    success: false,
    error: err.message || 'Something went wrong'
  });
});

To actually route an error to this handler, you call next(err) — passing an error object as next's argument signals to Express 'skip all remaining normal middleware and routes, and jump directly to the error-handling middleware instead':

app.get('/tasks/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const task = await findTaskOrThrow(req.params.id);
    res.json({ success: true, data: task });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err); // routes the error to the centralized error handler above
  }
});

This pattern — try/catch inside an async route handler, calling next(err) in the catch block — is the standard way to connect individual route-level errors into one centralized handling mechanism.

